            <ArticleTitle>Idiopathic Hypereosinophilic Syndrome With Cutaneous Manifestations and Flame Figures: A Spectrum of Eosinophilic Dermatoses Whose Features Overlap With Wells' Syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>910-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/DAD.0000000000000279</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPORTANCE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Wells syndrome (WS) (eosinophilic cellulitis) is an uncommon eosinophilic dermatitis that has been rarely described in association with, but distinct from, hypereosinophilic syndrome (HES).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBSERVATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a case of an eosinophilic dermatosis with flame figures in association with idiopathic HES, manifested by inflammatory myocarditis, asthma, and peripheral blood eosinophilia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS AND RELEVANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The diagnoses of WS and HES, rather than being distinct findings, may represent 2 entities on a spectrum of hypereosinophilic diseases. The diagnosis of WS should be made with caution and should prompt a thorough investigation that includes a work-up for a systemic eosinophilic disorder.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
